<header><p class="st-kicker">prince bishopric · Germany and Central Europe</p><h1 class="st-title">Imperial Abbey of Saint Emmeram</h1><p class="st-meta">1295 CE – 1803 CE (approx.) · capital Regensburg</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">One of Bavaria's oldest Benedictine abbeys, which became an imperial principality of its own inside the walls of Regensburg.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>The abbey grew up in the 8th century over the tomb of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saint-emmeram">Saint Emmeram</a>, a Frankish <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishop</a> martyred around 652, and for long its abbots were also bishops of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/free-imperial-city-of-regensburg">Regensburg</a>. It separated from the bishopric in 975 under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wolfgang-of-regensburg">Saint Wolfgang</a>. Its independence from Bavarian and episcopal control was confirmed by the king in 1295, and in 1731 its abbots were raised to princes of the empire.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The prince-abbot sat in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/imperial-diet">Imperial Diet</a>, which from 1663 met permanently in <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/free-imperial-city-of-regensburg">Regensburg</a>, yet his territory was little more than the abbey precinct and a few estates. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saint-emmeram">Saint Emmeram</a> was famous for its scriptorium and library, which held the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/codex-aureus-of-st-emmeram">Codex Aureus of Saint Emmeram</a>, and in the 18th century for scientific scholarship. It remained Catholic within a Lutheran free city, the Free Imperial City of Regensburg.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>In 1803 the abbey passed to the short-lived <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/principality-of-regensburg">Principality of Regensburg</a> under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karl-theodor-von-dalberg">Karl Theodor von Dalberg</a>; the monastery was dissolved after <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/free-imperial-city-of-regensburg">Regensburg</a> went to the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/kingdom-of-bavaria">Kingdom of Bavaria</a> in 1810. In 1812 the buildings became the residence of the princes of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/principality-of-thurn-and-taxis">Thurn und Taxis</a>, who still live there.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/imperial-abbey-of-saint-emmeram.jpg" alt="Imperial Abbey of Saint Emmeram" width="320" height="179"><figcaption>Image: Dr. Bernd Gross · CC BY-SA 3.0 de · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Schloss_St._Emmeram_Regensburg_32.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1295 CE – 1803 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/13th-century">13th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>prince bishopric</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>theocratic</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Regensburg</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Frobenius Forster</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Catholicism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Bavarian German</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/germany">Germany</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>St. Emmeram's Abbey</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1549&amp;focus=imperial-abbey-of-saint-emmeram" aria-label="Imperial Abbey of Saint Emmeram on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4959 467 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Imperial Abbey of Saint Emmeram lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5279 723L5229 694M5279 723L5279 723"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5229 694h0M5279 723h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5279 723h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5279 723h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks its recorded location. <a href="/atlas?year=1549&amp;focus=imperial-abbey-of-saint-emmeram">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
